# A value fallible twice, and the one reader that cannot say so.
#
# `m[k]` on a `{str: i64?}` is an `i64??`, and so is `find` over a `[i64?]`:
# nothing was WRITTEN to make it, so the parser's `nested_fallible` never fires
# — that refusal covers the written form alone (defect 051).
#
# Of §4.6's four one-level readers, three hand the second level back in their
# TYPE: `.must()` and `?` give an `i64?`, and `.default(v)` types `v` against
# the payload, so a wrong depth fails at its next use and the compiler says so
# there. **`.is_err()` returns a bare `bool`**, and the stored failure leaves
# the line unread — a plausible program that compiles, runs, and answers the
# question the author did not ask (defect 050).
#
# So the asymmetry is exactly one reader wide, and panel 160 refused exactly
# one reader. Refusing all four was measured and declined: it refuses correct
# programs, `tests/golden/check/a-fallible-type-is-never-written-fallible-twice.hero`
# among them, and the single mistake it catches that this does not is already
# an abort at 134 in the runtime's own handle ledger.
#
# The question this module asks is the one none of the readers asked: **what is
# the payload?**

use token
use ast
use source
use check/table
use check/state
use flow_errors

# Whether `payload` is itself a fallible type. A poisoned payload is `.fallible`
# too — `check/discard.hero` paid for that one, where a `T?` whose payload was
# poisoned printed as `??` on a line that already carried `cannot_infer` — so
# the caller asks `table.poisoned` first and this stays a pure shape question.
function is_nested(c: state.Checker, payload: i64) -> bool
    return match table.get(c.out.types, payload)
        .fallible                                                 => true
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit => false
        .array | .fixed | .map | .function_ty | .named            => false
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic | .error                   => false

# `.is_err()` where the payload is itself fallible. The answer would be about
# the OUTER level only and the `bool` it returns would not say so, so it is
# refused. The poisoned type goes back so nothing downstream reports it twice.
#
# One mistake, one report: a poisoned payload already carries its own
# diagnostic, and a value that is not nested is the ordinary case and answers
# `bool` as it always did.
function is_err_type(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], payload: i64, span: token.Span) -> i64
    if table.poisoned(c.out.types, payload) || !is_nested(c, payload)
        return table.bool_ty(c.out.types)
    inner = state.show(c, s, decls, payload)
    state.push_diagnostic(@c, flow_errors.nested_read(inner: inner, span: span))
    return state.error_ty_of(c)
